 ## Overview
-Stratified Ray Sampling is a technique that generates sample points along a camera ray by dividing the ray's depth interval into equal-sized bins and drawing one random sample uniformly from each bin. It solves the problem of aliasing and high variance inherent in pure uniform sampling by ensuring that the entire depth range is covered without gaps, while the random jitter within bins breaks up regular patterns [S4, unofficial][S5]. Unlike [Uniform Ray Sampling](1261-uniform-ray-sampling.md), which places points at fixed intervals, this method introduces controlled noise to improve the statistical properties of the integral estimate [S2, unofficial].
+Stratified Ray Sampling is a technique for distributing sample points along a camera ray in volume rendering by partitioning the ray's depth interval into equal-sized bins and drawing one sample uniformly at random from each bin. It solves the problem of aliasing and clustering inherent in pure uniform random sampling by guaranteeing one sample per spatial segment of the ray. It differs from [Stratified Monte Carlo Radiance Estimate](1373-stratified-monte-carlo-radiance-estimate.md) by focusing specifically on the geometric placement of samples along the ray depth rather than the statistical estimation of radiance integrals, and from [Batch Stratified Sampling](2730-batch-stratified-sampling.md) by operating on individual rays rather than optimizing batch-level variance reduction.
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs:**
-*   `origins`: Ray origins, shape $[N_{rays}, 3]$.
-*   `directions`: Ray directions, shape $[N_{rays}, 3]$.
-*   `min_depth` ($u_n$): Near plane distance.
-*   `max_depth` ($u_f$): Far plane distance.
-*   `n_pts_per_ray` ($N$): Number of samples per ray.
-*   `lindisp` (bool): If True, sample linearly in disparity ($1/u$) instead of depth [S4, unofficial].
-*   `perturb` (bool): If True, add uniform noise within each bin [S4, unofficial].
+*   Ray origin $\mathbf{o}$ (3D vector).
+*   Ray direction $\mathbf{d}$ (normalized 3D vector).
+*   Near depth $t_n$ (scalar).
+*   Far depth $t_f$ (scalar).
+*   Number of samples $k$ (integer).
+*   Perturbation flag `perturb` (boolean, default True).
+*   Linear disparity flag `lindisp` (boolean, default False).
 
 **Outputs:**
-*   `z_vals`: Sample depths, shape $[N_{rays}, N]$.
-*   `sample_points`: 3D coordinates, shape $[N_{rays}, N, 3]$.
+*   Array of sample depths $\boldsymbol{\tau}$ of size $k$.
+*   Array of 3D sample positions $\mathbf{P}$ of size $k \times 3$.
 
 **Data Structures:**
-*   `z_vals`: A tensor of floating-point values representing the distance from the camera origin along the ray direction for each sample point.
+*   `z_vals`: Tensor of shape $(k)$ storing the depth values.
+*   `t_rand`: Tensor of shape $(k)$ storing uniform random numbers $u_i \sim U[0, 1]$.
 
